      <section class="lf-article-section"><h2>Know exactly what a lead represents</h2><p>Cost per lead is campaign spending divided by attributed inquiries. It may include calls or submitted forms, but an inquiry does not necessarily mean the customer is reachable or within the company&#x27;s service territory.</p><p>Phone-link clicks, answered calls, forms, booked inspections and completed services are separate events. Report them separately so campaign performance is not overstated.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>Calculate cost per qualified lead</h2><p>Define a qualified lead using useful criteria: valid contact, service coverage, an actual pest-control need and a next step the business is capable of delivering.</p><p>For illustration only, a hypothetical $1,800 advertising campaign producing 45 inquiries costs $40 per inquiry. If 30 meet the business&#x27;s qualification rules, cost per qualified lead becomes $60. These are examples, not market benchmarks.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>Measure confirmed inspections and service bookings</h2><p>Booking an actual inspection is more meaningful than clicking a calendar link. Where possible, confirm the booking through the scheduling system and record whether the visit occurred.</p><p>If that hypothetical campaign results in 15 confirmed appointments, cost per booked appointment is $120. That number still does not establish how many customers purchased a service or whether their jobs were profitable.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>Separate different service economics</h2><p>Initial general pest treatments, termite work, rodent management and commercial pest contracts can have different sales cycles, labor requirements, material costs and potential values.</p><p>Evaluate acquisition costs by service rather than assigning one acceptable CPL to the entire business. A commercial prospect may justify a different sales effort from a standard residential service call.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>Account for ongoing and one-time costs</h2><p>Advertising is only one part of customer acquisition. Landing pages, creative, agency fees, software and staff response time may also affect the cost of gaining a customer.</p><p>Compare revenue after direct job costs, not just gross sales. If recurring plans are relevant, distinguish realized revenue from assumed future renewal value.</p></section>
<section class="lf-article-section"><h2>Use real outcomes before increasing ad spend</h2><p>Track campaign source, lead qualification, appointment attendance, completed jobs and contribution margin. Look for trends by pest type, territory and service.</p><p>Competitor activity, location, seasonal demand and operational capacity affect costs. Avoid using a single published industry CPL as a guarantee for the company&#x27;s market.</p></section>

